# How long a run waits for the interface before it gives up on it. Half of
# its own time budget, because what it is waiting for is another run of the
# same kind - and that one is bounded by the same budget. A fixed number
# cannot be right for both a 10-second and a 300-second test, and on a probe
# where two sensors share one radio it decides whether the second one
# measures or reports iface-busy.
#
# The ceiling is not a round number: the sensor script waits
# "--timeout plus 20 seconds" on the socket, and this run needs the whole
# of --timeout for the measurement itself. Waiting longer than those 20
# seconds would have the script time out while the helper is still working
# - a sensor error for a measurement that was about to succeed. 15 leaves
# five seconds for the handover itself.
LOCK_WAIT_CEILING = 15
LOCK_WAIT_SECONDS = 15

# How a job's time to live is derived from how often it is asked for, and
# the floor under it. See job_ttl. The floor is an hour because the PRTG
# scanning interval is the setting now: a job has to survive the gap to
# its second request, and the first gap after somebody raised the
# interval, before the helper has seen either. A deleted sensor costs at
# most an hour of measuring at its own pace.
JOB_TTL_FACTOR = 3
JOB_TTL_MINIMUM = 3600

# The helper plans the radio from what it sees (ADR 0014): how often PRTG
# asks for a job, and how long its runs really take. Neither is configured.
#
# The floor is the shortest interval at which one network is measured
# again, however often its sensor asks. With a floor of a second the radio
# signed in without a break, and an access point answered that with status
# 16 until nothing got through (seen on a probe with two jobs on one SSID
# asking every minute). A minute between two sign-ins of the same network
# keeps a sensor that asks often from hammering the access point.
REFRESH_FLOOR_SECONDS = 60.0

# The rest after every sign-in before the radio takes the next network. An
# access point sees one station leave and the next arrive from the same
# radio, and back to back within a second that is what it throttles. Part
# of every round, since the radio spends it whatever the run did.
RADIO_PAUSE_SECONDS = 10.0

# How many of its own intervals a job may go unasked before it is no
# longer measured. Changing a parameter of a sensor in PRTG makes it a
# different job, and the old one used to be measured on for an hour until
# it expired - an hour of a configuration nobody asks for any more, failing
# and taking airtime from the others. Three intervals stop it within
# minutes and still ride out a scan that came late. The job itself stays
# until it expires, so a sensor that asks again is measured at once.
